INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$7ce14583$SATQ$, $SATQ$07f9c472-31de-4d81-91c5-b0e774ff7102$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In Asiya Wadud&rsquo;s 2022 poem &ldquo;Shorn, Treaded Red,&rdquo; the poet contemplates a painting that has inspired her: Etel Adnan&rsquo;s 2020 work <em>Satellites 27</em>. The painting, which features overlapping geometric shapes, fuels the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in responding to Adnan&rsquo;s artwork, Wadud&rsquo;s poem reflects on the relationship between poetry and other art forms.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9a698860-a844-423c-9221-eeecb4fd1e05","content":"<p>In other words,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"bb8f5917-d8b2-416b-9fcf-dc431f474a07","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"b8da8807-5730-4ad7-b116-cd6308fad917","content":"<p>What&rsquo;s more,</p>"},{"id":"a16ee39e-3c2a-4fcb-bec7-041e0fde40b5","content":"<p>Conversely,&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b8da8807-5730-4ad7-b116-cd6308fad917"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "What&rsquo;s more" logically signals that this sentence introduces an additional aspect of Wadud&rsquo;s poem beyond what was previously discussed. While the previous sentence establishes that Adnan&rsquo;s painting has inspired "the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity," this sentence provides a separate, additional claim about the poem: that it reflects on poetry&rsquo;s relationship to other art forms.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "in other words" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a paraphrase or restatement of the previous point about how Adnan&rsquo;s painting has inspired "the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity." Instead, the sentence provides a separate, additional claim about the poem. Choice B is incorrect because "for instance" illogically signals that the information in this sentence supports the previous point about "the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity" by providing an example. Instead, the sentence provides a separate, additional claim about the poem. Choice D is incorrect because "conversely" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is contrary to the previous point about how Adnan&rsquo;s painting has inspired "the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity." Instead, the sentence provides a separate, additional claim about the poem.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7ce14583","external_id":"07f9c472-31de-4d81-91c5-b0e774ff7102","vaultid":null,"uId":"38c05583-e66e-4e2d-b7b7-dc8f35aea090","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In Asiya Wadud&rsquo;s 2022 poem &ldquo;Shorn, Treaded Red,&rdquo; the poet contemplates a painting that has inspired her: Etel Adnan&rsquo;s 2020 work <em>Satellites 27</em>. The painting, which features overlapping geometric shapes, fuels the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in responding to Adnan&rsquo;s artwork, Wadud&rsquo;s poem reflects on the relationship between poetry and other art forms.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9a698860-a844-423c-9221-eeecb4fd1e05","content":"<p>In other words,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"bb8f5917-d8b2-416b-9fcf-dc431f474a07","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"b8da8807-5730-4ad7-b116-cd6308fad917","content":"<p>What&rsquo;s more,</p>"},{"id":"a16ee39e-3c2a-4fcb-bec7-041e0fde40b5","content":"<p>Conversely,&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["b8da8807-5730-4ad7-b116-cd6308fad917"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"What&rsquo;s more\" logically signals that this sentence introduces an additional aspect of Wadud&rsquo;s poem beyond what was previously discussed. While the previous sentence establishes that Adnan&rsquo;s painting has inspired \"the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity,\" this sentence provides a separate, additional claim about the poem: that it reflects on poetry&rsquo;s relationship to other art forms.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"in other words\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a paraphrase or restatement of the previous point about how Adnan&rsquo;s painting has inspired \"the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity.\" Instead, the sentence provides a separate, additional claim about the poem. Choice B is incorrect because \"for instance\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence supports the previous point about \"the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity\" by providing an example. Instead, the sentence provides a separate, additional claim about the poem. Choice D is incorrect because \"conversely\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is contrary to the previous point about how Adnan&rsquo;s painting has inspired \"the poem&rsquo;s exploration of temporality and identity.\" Instead, the sentence provides a separate, additional claim about the poem.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$7d56630a$SATQ$, $SATQ$d9ad8fc2-d0e5-4aba-8321-ffaa68ab45d4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0e3094bc-2544-4d74-b091-d67ec73d4198$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In studying whether jellyfish sleep, researchers Michael Abrams, Claire Bedbrook, and Ravi Nath attempted to answer three questions. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> is there a period each day when the pulse rates of jellyfish decline? Second, do jellyfish respond more slowly to stimuli during that period? Finally, if prevented from sleeping, are jellyfish adversely affected?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"639714d6-5ee0-4a38-a644-c9be465e51b6","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"26fe574d-619a-4e85-832d-32a8b483e388","content":"<p>First,</p>"},{"id":"fb0dfacd-b847-43c6-864e-5ac47131db1d","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"c2a73c39-2629-4720-bcd2-39d45789b912","content":"<p>However,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["26fe574d-619a-4e85-832d-32a8b483e388"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;First&rdquo; logically signals that the question in this sentence&mdash;whether there is a daily period during which jellyfish pulse rates decline&mdash;is the first in a sequence of three questions the researchers attempted to answer about jellyfish sleep behavior. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; illogically signals that the question in this sentence is a result of the three questions the researchers attempted to answer. Instead, it is the first of those three questions. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;additionally&rdquo; illogically signals that the question in this sentence is an additional question related to the three questions the researchers attempted to answer. Instead, it is the first of those three questions. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the question in this sentence contrasts with the three questions the researchers attempted to answer. Instead, it is the first of those three questions. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-261-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3290df90-6ebb-42c1-b0fa-25301b8efce1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7d56630a","external_id":"d9ad8fc2-d0e5-4aba-8321-ffaa68ab45d4","vaultid":"0e3094bc-2544-4d74-b091-d67ec73d4198","uId":"671f2fb8-b65f-4870-b2e5-4a0043292ef5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In studying whether jellyfish sleep, researchers Michael Abrams, Claire Bedbrook, and Ravi Nath attempted to answer three questions. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> is there a period each day when the pulse rates of jellyfish decline? Second, do jellyfish respond more slowly to stimuli during that period? Finally, if prevented from sleeping, are jellyfish adversely affected?</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"639714d6-5ee0-4a38-a644-c9be465e51b6","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"26fe574d-619a-4e85-832d-32a8b483e388","content":"<p>First,</p>"},{"id":"fb0dfacd-b847-43c6-864e-5ac47131db1d","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"c2a73c39-2629-4720-bcd2-39d45789b912","content":"<p>However,</p>"}],"keys":["26fe574d-619a-4e85-832d-32a8b483e388"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;First&rdquo; logically signals that the question in this sentence&mdash;whether there is a daily period during which jellyfish pulse rates decline&mdash;is the first in a sequence of three questions the researchers attempted to answer about jellyfish sleep behavior. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;as a result&rdquo; illogically signals that the question in this sentence is a result of the three questions the researchers attempted to answer. Instead, it is the first of those three questions. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;additionally&rdquo; illogically signals that the question in this sentence is an additional question related to the three questions the researchers attempted to answer. Instead, it is the first of those three questions. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;however&rdquo; illogically signals that the question in this sentence contrasts with the three questions the researchers attempted to answer. Instead, it is the first of those three questions. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-261-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3290df90-6ebb-42c1-b0fa-25301b8efce1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959334,"updateDate":1691007959334}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959334, 1691007959334),
    ($SATQ$7d5c32e6$SATQ$, $SATQ$8a56d1a4-d07b-451f-b4f9-1b644a8f0a5b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The fifth Solvay Conference on Physics was held in 1927.</li>
<li>It brought together twenty-nine of the era&rsquo;s preeminent scientists to discuss the emerging field of quantum theory.</li>
<li>The conference famously featured a debate between physicists Albert Einstein and Niels Bohr.</li>
<li>Bohr proposed that subatomic entities like electrons had only probable realities until they were observed.</li>
<li>Einstein argued that subatomic entities like electrons had a reality independent of observation.</li>
<li>Bohr&rsquo;s position, later called the Copenhagen interpretation, remains the most widely accepted theory of quantum mechanics.&nbsp;</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to place Einstein&rsquo;s argument within its historical context.&nbsp;Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"920b6b95-c2c4-4f26-8d2a-c2ff02f4a1e7","content":"<p>During the dawn of quantum theory, Einstein maintained the independent reality of some subatomic entities, although Bohr&rsquo;s opposing interpretation would become the widely accepted view.</p>"},{"id":"8c38d225-6311-47e4-bdf5-0e32043885db","content":"<p>At the 1927 Solvay Conference on Physics, Einstein disagreed with Bohr&rsquo;s argument that subatomic entities like electrons had a reality independent of observation.</p>"},{"id":"d462bb76-5c10-4891-94ec-494b30ea8941","content":"<p>The attendees of the 1927 Solvay Conference were among the preeminent scientists of their era, including Einstein, who opposed Bohr&rsquo;s proposal.</p>"},{"id":"37939486-c646-4bf1-819a-4685dd71eaae","content":"<p>In 1927, Einstein and Bohr engaged in a famous debate; Bohr&rsquo;s argument, later called the Copenhagen interpretation, would remain popular decades after.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["920b6b95-c2c4-4f26-8d2a-c2ff02f4a1e7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence places Einstein&rsquo;s argument within the historical context of the development of quantum theory, noting that his argument&mdash;made during the dawn of the field&mdash;conflicted with Bohr&rsquo;s argument, which became the widely accepted view.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents information from the notes, attributing the argument that electrons had a reality independent of observation to Bohr, not Einstein. In addition, while the sentence provides the date of the conference, it doesn&rsquo;t place Einstein&rsquo;s argument in the context of the development of quantum theory. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Einstein attended the 1927 Solvay Conference; it doesn&rsquo;t identify Einstein&rsquo;s argument or place it in the historical context of the development of quantum theory. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence explains that Einstein and Bohr had a famous debate in 1927 and that Bohr&rsquo;s argument remained popular decades afterward; it doesn&rsquo;t identify Einstein&rsquo;s argument or place it in the context of the development of quantum theory.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7d5c32e6","external_id":"8a56d1a4-d07b-451f-b4f9-1b644a8f0a5b","vaultid":null,"uId":"21e6f58b-e8f8-420f-a6e8-ff3d513ca488","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The fifth Solvay Conference on Physics was held in 1927.</li>\n<li>It brought together twenty-nine of the era&rsquo;s preeminent scientists to discuss the emerging field of quantum theory.</li>\n<li>The conference famously featured a debate between physicists Albert Einstein and Niels Bohr.</li>\n<li>Bohr proposed that subatomic entities like electrons had only probable realities until they were observed.</li>\n<li>Einstein argued that subatomic entities like electrons had a reality independent of observation.</li>\n<li>Bohr&rsquo;s position, later called the Copenhagen interpretation, remains the most widely accepted theory of quantum mechanics.&nbsp;</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to place Einstein&rsquo;s argument within its historical context.&nbsp;Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"920b6b95-c2c4-4f26-8d2a-c2ff02f4a1e7","content":"<p>During the dawn of quantum theory, Einstein maintained the independent reality of some subatomic entities, although Bohr&rsquo;s opposing interpretation would become the widely accepted view.</p>"},{"id":"8c38d225-6311-47e4-bdf5-0e32043885db","content":"<p>At the 1927 Solvay Conference on Physics, Einstein disagreed with Bohr&rsquo;s argument that subatomic entities like electrons had a reality independent of observation.</p>"},{"id":"d462bb76-5c10-4891-94ec-494b30ea8941","content":"<p>The attendees of the 1927 Solvay Conference were among the preeminent scientists of their era, including Einstein, who opposed Bohr&rsquo;s proposal.</p>"},{"id":"37939486-c646-4bf1-819a-4685dd71eaae","content":"<p>In 1927, Einstein and Bohr engaged in a famous debate; Bohr&rsquo;s argument, later called the Copenhagen interpretation, would remain popular decades after.</p>"}],"keys":["920b6b95-c2c4-4f26-8d2a-c2ff02f4a1e7"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence places Einstein&rsquo;s argument within the historical context of the development of quantum theory, noting that his argument&mdash;made during the dawn of the field&mdash;conflicted with Bohr&rsquo;s argument, which became the widely accepted view.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents information from the notes, attributing the argument that electrons had a reality independent of observation to Bohr, not Einstein. In addition, while the sentence provides the date of the conference, it doesn&rsquo;t place Einstein&rsquo;s argument in the context of the development of quantum theory. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Einstein attended the 1927 Solvay Conference; it doesn&rsquo;t identify Einstein&rsquo;s argument or place it in the historical context of the development of quantum theory. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence explains that Einstein and Bohr had a famous debate in 1927 and that Bohr&rsquo;s argument remained popular decades afterward; it doesn&rsquo;t identify Einstein&rsquo;s argument or place it in the context of the development of quantum theory.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669211,"updateDate":1743430669211}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669211, 1743430669211),
    ($SATQ$7f20374f$SATQ$, $SATQ$6fedc856-4ddb-4175-97ec-9582fbf3e4e2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The 435 state districts in the US House of Representatives are designed to be roughly equal in population. These districts average around 760,000 people in size. However, only about 730,000 people live in the state of Alaska. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Alaska has just one House district, encompassing the entire state.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"62feaa8b-4f36-46ff-b589-d07480158273","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"60de4272-c609-4d61-801f-ce0d79fddbd7","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"2d3d3843-f659-4d43-8a6c-0f2544fd4aca","content":"<p>Finally,</p>"},{"id":"d2ff5979-f7c4-43f3-84cd-c1f498838fb1","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["62feaa8b-4f36-46ff-b589-d07480158273"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. "As a result" logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that, in the US House of Representatives, the entire state of Alaska has just one district&mdash;is the result or consequence of Alaska having only about 730,000 people. This is slightly below the average for House districts, which are designed to have roughly equal populations.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because "instead" illogically signals that Alaska having just one state district is an alternative to the information about the state&rsquo;s population in the previous sentence. Rather, the fact that Alaska has just one district is a result or consequence of the entire state having slightly fewer people than an average-sized district. Choice C is incorrect because "finally" illogically signals that the information in this sentence about Alaska having just one district indicates a last step in a process or a concluding summary. Instead, the fact that Alaska has just one district is a result or consequence of the entire state having slightly fewer people than an average-sized district. Choice D is incorrect because "for instance" illogically signals that the information in this sentence about Alaska having just one district exemplifies the information about the state&rsquo;s population in the previous sentence. Instead, the fact that Alaska has just one district is a result or consequence of the entire state having slightly fewer people than an average-sized district.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7f20374f","external_id":"6fedc856-4ddb-4175-97ec-9582fbf3e4e2","vaultid":null,"uId":"4920fe27-b8b8-4939-a6ab-9f97f66d7388","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The 435 state districts in the US House of Representatives are designed to be roughly equal in population. These districts average around 760,000 people in size. However, only about 730,000 people live in the state of Alaska. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Alaska has just one House district, encompassing the entire state.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"62feaa8b-4f36-46ff-b589-d07480158273","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"60de4272-c609-4d61-801f-ce0d79fddbd7","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"2d3d3843-f659-4d43-8a6c-0f2544fd4aca","content":"<p>Finally,</p>"},{"id":"d2ff5979-f7c4-43f3-84cd-c1f498838fb1","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"}],"keys":["62feaa8b-4f36-46ff-b589-d07480158273"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. \"As a result\" logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that, in the US House of Representatives, the entire state of Alaska has just one district&mdash;is the result or consequence of Alaska having only about 730,000 people. This is slightly below the average for House districts, which are designed to have roughly equal populations.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because \"instead\" illogically signals that Alaska having just one state district is an alternative to the information about the state&rsquo;s population in the previous sentence. Rather, the fact that Alaska has just one district is a result or consequence of the entire state having slightly fewer people than an average-sized district. Choice C is incorrect because \"finally\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence about Alaska having just one district indicates a last step in a process or a concluding summary. Instead, the fact that Alaska has just one district is a result or consequence of the entire state having slightly fewer people than an average-sized district. Choice D is incorrect because \"for instance\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence about Alaska having just one district exemplifies the information about the state&rsquo;s population in the previous sentence. Instead, the fact that Alaska has just one district is a result or consequence of the entire state having slightly fewer people than an average-sized district.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323044,"updateDate":1730147323044}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323044, 1730147323044),
    ($SATQ$7f2781fd$SATQ$, $SATQ$ac6011e6-00d4-46f9-99f9-ca64c8117fe0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Certhidea olivacea</em> is a perching bird that can be found on the Gal&aacute;pagos Island of Pinz&oacute;n.</li>
<li><em>Creagrus furcatus</em> is a seabird that can be found on the Gal&aacute;pagos Island of Darwin.&nbsp;</li>
<li>Conservation organizations evaluate the risk that species will become extinct in the near future.</li>
<li><em>C. olivacea</em> faces a high risk of extinction.</li>
<li><em>C. furcatus</em> faces little risk of becoming extinct in the near future.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to compare the extinction risk faced by <em>C. olivacea</em> with that faced by <em>C. furcatus</em>. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8f15bf97-18fb-43cf-a52d-c34df5597a45","content":"<p>According to conservation organizations, <em>C. olivacea</em> has a higher risk of becoming extinct in the near future than <em>C. furcatus</em>.</p>"},{"id":"f90b1403-91f5-4254-b943-31253f15f533","content":"<p><em>C. furcatus</em> faces a high risk of extinction, while <em>C. olivacea</em> faces little risk of becoming extinct in the near future.</p>"},{"id":"bb81031e-62e6-41ed-bc16-66985c58687f","content":"<p>Conservation organizations have evaluated both <em>C. furcatus</em>&rsquo;s and <em>C. olivacea</em>&rsquo;s risk of becoming extinct in the near future.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"20d5df74-480e-4819-b741-992a32611b7b","content":"<p><em>C. olivacea</em> is a perching bird that faces a high risk of extinction.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8f15bf97-18fb-43cf-a52d-c34df5597a45"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence compares the extinction risks of the two species, noting that <em>C. olivacea</em> has a higher risk of becoming extinct in the near future than <em>C. furcatus</em>.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence does compare the extinction risks of the two species, it misrepresents information from the notes; <em>C. olivacea</em> faces a higher risk of becoming extinct in the near future than <em>C. furcatus</em>, not the other way around. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence mentions that conservation organizations have evaluated both species for extinction risk, it doesn&rsquo;t compare the extinction risks of the two species. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence states that <em>C. olivacea</em> faces a high risk of extinction, it doesn&rsquo;t mention <em>C. furcatus</em> or compare the extinction risks of the two species.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7f2781fd","external_id":"ac6011e6-00d4-46f9-99f9-ca64c8117fe0","vaultid":null,"uId":"f08e9ed9-6ef0-4a11-b63f-513bc38ef4b8","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Certhidea olivacea</em> is a perching bird that can be found on the Gal&aacute;pagos Island of Pinz&oacute;n.</li>\n<li><em>Creagrus furcatus</em> is a seabird that can be found on the Gal&aacute;pagos Island of Darwin.&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Conservation organizations evaluate the risk that species will become extinct in the near future.</li>\n<li><em>C. olivacea</em> faces a high risk of extinction.</li>\n<li><em>C. furcatus</em> faces little risk of becoming extinct in the near future.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to compare the extinction risk faced by <em>C. olivacea</em> with that faced by <em>C. furcatus</em>. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8f15bf97-18fb-43cf-a52d-c34df5597a45","content":"<p>According to conservation organizations, <em>C. olivacea</em> has a higher risk of becoming extinct in the near future than <em>C. furcatus</em>.</p>"},{"id":"f90b1403-91f5-4254-b943-31253f15f533","content":"<p><em>C. furcatus</em> faces a high risk of extinction, while <em>C. olivacea</em> faces little risk of becoming extinct in the near future.</p>"},{"id":"bb81031e-62e6-41ed-bc16-66985c58687f","content":"<p>Conservation organizations have evaluated both <em>C. furcatus</em>&rsquo;s and <em>C. olivacea</em>&rsquo;s risk of becoming extinct in the near future.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"20d5df74-480e-4819-b741-992a32611b7b","content":"<p><em>C. olivacea</em> is a perching bird that faces a high risk of extinction.</p>"}],"keys":["8f15bf97-18fb-43cf-a52d-c34df5597a45"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence compares the extinction risks of the two species, noting that <em>C. olivacea</em> has a higher risk of becoming extinct in the near future than <em>C. furcatus</em>.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence does compare the extinction risks of the two species, it misrepresents information from the notes; <em>C. olivacea</em> faces a higher risk of becoming extinct in the near future than <em>C. furcatus</em>, not the other way around. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence mentions that conservation organizations have evaluated both species for extinction risk, it doesn&rsquo;t compare the extinction risks of the two species. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence states that <em>C. olivacea</em> faces a high risk of extinction, it doesn&rsquo;t mention <em>C. furcatus</em> or compare the extinction risks of the two species.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061190,"updateDate":1755117061190}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061190, 1755117061190),
    ($SATQ$7fa2b1ee$SATQ$, $SATQ$30b9dac9-36d2-488e-a95f-ae0404e84c5f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In meteorology, an air mass is a large body of air with generally uniform humidity and temperature.</li>
<li>Air masses are commonly classified by two-letter names.</li>
<li>The first letter indicates the humidity of the air mass, while the second letter indicates the temperature.</li>
<li>cA (continental arctic) means dry and cold, for example.</li>
<li>mT (maritime tropical) means moist and warm.</li>
<li>This classification system is based on the work of a Swedish meteorologist named Tor Bergeron (1891&ndash;1977).</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to provide an example of an air mass. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"13e818de-787b-4c5f-9788-d172df03db89","content":"<p>Air masses are large bodies of air with generally uniform humidity and temperature.</p>"},{"id":"df039e50-efa2-4f8d-b9eb-3840502f5ffe","content":"<p>The air mass classification system uses two-letter names and is based on the work of Tor Bergeron, a Swedish meteorologist.</p>"},{"id":"96ffce18-9855-4284-b7cc-b1472d7d1da5","content":"<p>Air masses are commonly classified by a two-letter name that indicates humidity and temperature.</p>"},{"id":"5e1a385d-b325-4f5e-ae92-67e77dc6d6ee","content":"<p>One type of air mass is known as a cA, or continental arctic, air mass because it is dry and cold.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5e1a385d-b325-4f5e-ae92-67e77dc6d6ee"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence provides an example of an air mass: the cA, or continental arctic, air mass.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence provides a general definition of air masses; it doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of a specific air mass. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes the system used to classify air masses; it doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of a specific air mass. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence explains how air masses are classified; it doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of a specific air mass.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7fa2b1ee","external_id":"30b9dac9-36d2-488e-a95f-ae0404e84c5f","vaultid":null,"uId":"fb6e3f98-6364-41d1-8771-471b4e56ec82","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In meteorology, an air mass is a large body of air with generally uniform humidity and temperature.</li>\n<li>Air masses are commonly classified by two-letter names.</li>\n<li>The first letter indicates the humidity of the air mass, while the second letter indicates the temperature.</li>\n<li>cA (continental arctic) means dry and cold, for example.</li>\n<li>mT (maritime tropical) means moist and warm.</li>\n<li>This classification system is based on the work of a Swedish meteorologist named Tor Bergeron (1891&ndash;1977).</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to provide an example of an air mass. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"13e818de-787b-4c5f-9788-d172df03db89","content":"<p>Air masses are large bodies of air with generally uniform humidity and temperature.</p>"},{"id":"df039e50-efa2-4f8d-b9eb-3840502f5ffe","content":"<p>The air mass classification system uses two-letter names and is based on the work of Tor Bergeron, a Swedish meteorologist.</p>"},{"id":"96ffce18-9855-4284-b7cc-b1472d7d1da5","content":"<p>Air masses are commonly classified by a two-letter name that indicates humidity and temperature.</p>"},{"id":"5e1a385d-b325-4f5e-ae92-67e77dc6d6ee","content":"<p>One type of air mass is known as a cA, or continental arctic, air mass because it is dry and cold.</p>"}],"keys":["5e1a385d-b325-4f5e-ae92-67e77dc6d6ee"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence provides an example of an air mass: the cA, or continental arctic, air mass.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence provides a general definition of air masses; it doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of a specific air mass. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes the system used to classify air masses; it doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of a specific air mass. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence explains how air masses are classified; it doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of a specific air mass.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323035,"updateDate":1730147323035}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323035, 1730147323035),
    ($SATQ$7fd39a42$SATQ$, $SATQ$984f9ccb-46a2-4144-8163-ee2eb1dbe818$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4c5ebb70-2ae0-42d1-86cb-bbe9700d5d8e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Circular particle accelerators known as synchrotrons radiate energy in the form of light.</li>
<li>Synchrotron light is among the brightest light ever produced.</li>
<li>Synchrotron light is an ideal tool for researchers investigating the structure of matter.</li>
<li>The first synchrotron created for the purpose of providing synchrotron light was built in 1968.</li>
<li>It was called Tantalus and was housed near the University of Wisconsin&ndash;Madison.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the location of the first synchrotron built to provide synchrotron light. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d7956bd4-85ca-44ac-96a5-f1d3fa42ca64","content":"<p>Tantalus, the first synchrotron created for the purpose of providing synchrotron light, was built in 1968.</p>"},{"id":"d340478f-e600-450a-945f-248c96401c89","content":"<p>Circular particle accelerators known as synchrotrons radiate energy in the form of light, and this light is an ideal tool for researchers investigating the structure of matter.</p>"},{"id":"b8345d5c-2a81-4331-bd07-b6158fb062a0","content":"<p>The first synchrotron created for the purpose of providing synchrotron light, Tantalus, was housed near the University of Wisconsin&ndash;Madison.</p>"},{"id":"cdedcfce-08a9-4bb7-8231-31a11aa6ed4d","content":"<p>Synchrotron light is among the brightest light ever produced, making it an ideal tool for researchers investigating the structure of matter.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b8345d5c-2a81-4331-bd07-b6158fb062a0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. After identifying Tantalus as the first synchrotron built to provide light, the sentence emphasizes its location. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence identifies Tantalus as the first synchrotron built to provide light, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize (or mention) its location. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes synchrotrons and how researchers use them; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize (or mention) the location of Tantalus. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence describes synchrotron light and how researchers use it; the sentence doesn&rsquo;t emphasize (or mention) the location of Tantalus. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-021-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$8a686df3-1175-4ca7-aa1c-3acd10cfb6d2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7fd39a42","external_id":"984f9ccb-46a2-4144-8163-ee2eb1dbe818","vaultid":"4c5ebb70-2ae0-42d1-86cb-bbe9700d5d8e","uId":"a7ae4613-635f-4eb0-a17b-92b37ecb6d17","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Circular particle accelerators known as synchrotrons radiate energy in the form of light.</li>\n<li>Synchrotron light is among the brightest light ever produced.</li>\n<li>Synchrotron light is an ideal tool for researchers investigating the structure of matter.</li>\n<li>The first synchrotron created for the purpose of providing synchrotron light was built in 1968.</li>\n<li>It was called Tantalus and was housed near the University of Wisconsin&ndash;Madison.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the location of the first synchrotron built to provide synchrotron light. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d7956bd4-85ca-44ac-96a5-f1d3fa42ca64","content":"<p>Tantalus, the first synchrotron created for the purpose of providing synchrotron light, was built in 1968.</p>"},{"id":"d340478f-e600-450a-945f-248c96401c89","content":"<p>Circular particle accelerators known as synchrotrons radiate energy in the form of light, and this light is an ideal tool for researchers investigating the structure of matter.</p>"},{"id":"b8345d5c-2a81-4331-bd07-b6158fb062a0","content":"<p>The first synchrotron created for the purpose of providing synchrotron light, Tantalus, was housed near the University of Wisconsin&ndash;Madison.</p>"},{"id":"cdedcfce-08a9-4bb7-8231-31a11aa6ed4d","content":"<p>Synchrotron light is among the brightest light ever produced, making it an ideal tool for researchers investigating the structure of matter.</p>"}],"keys":["b8345d5c-2a81-4331-bd07-b6158fb062a0"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. After identifying Tantalus as the first synchrotron built to provide light, the sentence emphasizes its location. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence identifies Tantalus as the first synchrotron built to provide light, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize (or mention) its location. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes synchrotrons and how researchers use them; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize (or mention) the location of Tantalus. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence describes synchrotron light and how researchers use it; the sentence doesn&rsquo;t emphasize (or mention) the location of Tantalus. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-021-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"8a686df3-1175-4ca7-aa1c-3acd10cfb6d2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959317,"updateDate":1691007959317}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959317, 1691007959317),
    ($SATQ$804928b6$SATQ$, $SATQ$db2683eb-5c06-4ebc-bb3d-dba38ed428f5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Mary Kang is a Korean American portrait photographer.</li>
<li>She is based in New York City and in Austin, Texas.</li>
<li>One of Kang&rsquo;s photographs features artist Dominique Fung.</li>
<li>In the portrait, Fung is seated on the floor.</li>
<li>Five of Fung&rsquo;s paintings are resting against the wall behind her.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to describe where Fung is in the photograph to an audience already familiar with Kang and Fung. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f5efae4f-56ae-4b87-97b9-7975a0661ae4","content":"<p>Dominique Fung is in a photograph by Mary Kang, a portrait photographer based in New York City and Austin, Texas.</p>"},{"id":"74ebbe03-5e0f-4933-b6fe-58b76b1b7e83","content":"<p>Mary Kang is a photographer based in both New York City and Austin, Texas.</p>"},{"id":"9b32e08c-eff6-43ef-87ec-5f96a72a0ac2","content":"<p>Five paintings by artist Dominique Fung can be seen in the background of Mary Kang&rsquo;s photograph.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"4d1bbd4e-8757-4093-9232-6aa93db2a42f","content":"<p>In Kang&rsquo;s portrait of her, Fung is seated on the floor, with five of her paintings resting against the wall behind her.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4d1bbd4e-8757-4093-9232-6aa93db2a42f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence describes Fung&rsquo;s location in Kang&rsquo;s photograph, noting that Fung is seated on the floor. Additionally, because the sentence is intended for an audience already familiar with the artists, it omits the artists&rsquo; first names and other biographical information about them.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Fung appears in Kang&rsquo;s photograph; it doesn&rsquo;t describe Fung&rsquo;s location in Kang&rsquo;s photograph. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence identifies Kang and where she is based; it doesn&rsquo;t describe Fung&rsquo;s location in Kang&rsquo;s photograph. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes the background of Kang&rsquo;s photograph; it doesn&rsquo;t describe Fung&rsquo;s location in Kang&rsquo;s photograph.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"804928b6","external_id":"db2683eb-5c06-4ebc-bb3d-dba38ed428f5","vaultid":null,"uId":"8c8c3ed5-528f-487e-8e52-dd30f612781d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mary Kang is a Korean American portrait photographer.</li>\n<li>She is based in New York City and in Austin, Texas.</li>\n<li>One of Kang&rsquo;s photographs features artist Dominique Fung.</li>\n<li>In the portrait, Fung is seated on the floor.</li>\n<li>Five of Fung&rsquo;s paintings are resting against the wall behind her.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to describe where Fung is in the photograph to an audience already familiar with Kang and Fung. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f5efae4f-56ae-4b87-97b9-7975a0661ae4","content":"<p>Dominique Fung is in a photograph by Mary Kang, a portrait photographer based in New York City and Austin, Texas.</p>"},{"id":"74ebbe03-5e0f-4933-b6fe-58b76b1b7e83","content":"<p>Mary Kang is a photographer based in both New York City and Austin, Texas.</p>"},{"id":"9b32e08c-eff6-43ef-87ec-5f96a72a0ac2","content":"<p>Five paintings by artist Dominique Fung can be seen in the background of Mary Kang&rsquo;s photograph.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"4d1bbd4e-8757-4093-9232-6aa93db2a42f","content":"<p>In Kang&rsquo;s portrait of her, Fung is seated on the floor, with five of her paintings resting against the wall behind her.</p>"}],"keys":["4d1bbd4e-8757-4093-9232-6aa93db2a42f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence describes Fung&rsquo;s location in Kang&rsquo;s photograph, noting that Fung is seated on the floor. Additionally, because the sentence is intended for an audience already familiar with the artists, it omits the artists&rsquo; first names and other biographical information about them.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Fung appears in Kang&rsquo;s photograph; it doesn&rsquo;t describe Fung&rsquo;s location in Kang&rsquo;s photograph. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence identifies Kang and where she is based; it doesn&rsquo;t describe Fung&rsquo;s location in Kang&rsquo;s photograph. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes the background of Kang&rsquo;s photograph; it doesn&rsquo;t describe Fung&rsquo;s location in Kang&rsquo;s photograph.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323031,"updateDate":1730147323031}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323031, 1730147323031),
    ($SATQ$8112b7e3$SATQ$, $SATQ$9467e8c0-5715-4578-949a-d66ad4e1aa78$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ugandan American professor Peter Nazareth believed that Elvis Presley&rsquo;s music is best understood not as a homogeneous collection but as an anthology (because Elvis showcased the contributions of a wide range of gospel, blues, and rock artists). <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Nazareth entitled his college course on Elvis and his music, which focused on Elvis&rsquo;s many musical influences, &ldquo;Elvis as Anthology.&rdquo;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0a644bf2-ad3a-447f-b363-d8278bcde6e2","content":"<p>To that end,</p>"},{"id":"68a74a2a-56ba-4290-8374-cb59ccf28e17","content":"<p>In sum,</p>"},{"id":"af100b68-1681-497f-8152-0abfaf8d4e99","content":"<p>That is,</p>"},{"id":"773c06b4-43cd-46fe-8ae5-e0b82528a766","content":"<p>In addition,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0a644bf2-ad3a-447f-b363-d8278bcde6e2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. "To that end" logically signals that the activity described in this sentence&mdash;Nazareth titling his course "Elvis as Anthology"&mdash;is meant to further Nazareth&rsquo;s goal of helping others understand Presley&rsquo;s music as an anthology with a wide range of influences.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. "In sum" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence summarizes Nazareth&rsquo;s view of Presley&rsquo;s music as an anthology. Instead, titling his course "Elvis as Anthology" is a way for him to promote this view. Choice C is incorrect. "That is" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence is a clarification or interpretation of Nazareth&rsquo;s view of Presley&rsquo;s music as an anthology. Instead, titling his course "Elvis as Anthology" is a way for him to promote this view. Choice D is incorrect. "In addition" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence is merely an additional fact about Nazareth. Instead, titling his course "Elvis as Anthology" is a way for him to promote his view of Presley&rsquo;s music as an anthology.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"8112b7e3","external_id":"9467e8c0-5715-4578-949a-d66ad4e1aa78","vaultid":null,"uId":"3558607d-8975-44df-ad7e-9518520009c1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Ugandan American professor Peter Nazareth believed that Elvis Presley&rsquo;s music is best understood not as a homogeneous collection but as an anthology (because Elvis showcased the contributions of a wide range of gospel, blues, and rock artists). <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Nazareth entitled his college course on Elvis and his music, which focused on Elvis&rsquo;s many musical influences, &ldquo;Elvis as Anthology.&rdquo;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0a644bf2-ad3a-447f-b363-d8278bcde6e2","content":"<p>To that end,</p>"},{"id":"68a74a2a-56ba-4290-8374-cb59ccf28e17","content":"<p>In sum,</p>"},{"id":"af100b68-1681-497f-8152-0abfaf8d4e99","content":"<p>That is,</p>"},{"id":"773c06b4-43cd-46fe-8ae5-e0b82528a766","content":"<p>In addition,</p>"}],"keys":["0a644bf2-ad3a-447f-b363-d8278bcde6e2"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. \"To that end\" logically signals that the activity described in this sentence&mdash;Nazareth titling his course \"Elvis as Anthology\"&mdash;is meant to further Nazareth&rsquo;s goal of helping others understand Presley&rsquo;s music as an anthology with a wide range of influences.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. \"In sum\" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence summarizes Nazareth&rsquo;s view of Presley&rsquo;s music as an anthology. Instead, titling his course \"Elvis as Anthology\" is a way for him to promote this view. Choice C is incorrect. \"That is\" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence is a clarification or interpretation of Nazareth&rsquo;s view of Presley&rsquo;s music as an anthology. Instead, titling his course \"Elvis as Anthology\" is a way for him to promote this view. Choice D is incorrect. \"In addition\" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence is merely an additional fact about Nazareth. Instead, titling his course \"Elvis as Anthology\" is a way for him to promote his view of Presley&rsquo;s music as an anthology.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323044,"updateDate":1730147323044}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323044, 1730147323044),
    ($SATQ$81315093$SATQ$, $SATQ$5da5eb80-95a2-420c-a315-decc614a7775$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$24dbddaa-2c14-4f0e-8189-ac6816449312$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>A marathon is a long-distance running race that is 26.2 miles long.</li>
<li>An ultramarathon is a long-distance running race of more than 26.2 miles.</li>
<li>The Kepler Challenge is a one-day, 37.3-mile ultramarathon in New Zealand.</li>
<li>The Spreelauf is a six-day, 261-mile ultramarathon in Germany.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to make a generalization about ultramarathons. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d4e03f0a-c538-4752-b0f4-39f1623df16d","content":"<p>Examples of ultramarathons include the 37.3-mile Kepler Challenge in New Zealand and the 261-mile Spreelauf in Germany.</p>"},{"id":"bc403ebb-71dc-4b19-bd88-945ebfb1eb6d","content":"<p>A marathon is 26.2 miles long, but the Spreelauf ultramarathon, at 261 miles, is far longer.</p>"},{"id":"4872fe45-c48b-42dc-9779-df6bc08a0ce5","content":"<p>Ultramarathons range widely in length, from a few dozen miles to a few hundred.</p>"},{"id":"b62c61ec-39cb-40b5-8c34-b81929304a06","content":"<p>While the Kepler Challenge is a one-day ultramarathon, the Spreelauf is a six-day ultramarathon.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4872fe45-c48b-42dc-9779-df6bc08a0ce5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. This is the only choice that makes a generalization about ultramarathons. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice gives specific examples of ultramarathons but doesn&rsquo;t say anything about them as a category. Choice B is incorrect. This choice compares marathons in general to one specific ultramarathon but doesn&rsquo;t say anything about ultramarathons as a category. Choice D is incorrect. This choice contrasts two specific ultramarathons but doesn&rsquo;t say anything about them as a category. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-SYN-M007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b37a2d06-cb10-482a-b784-20ab8cb333a5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"81315093","external_id":"5da5eb80-95a2-420c-a315-decc614a7775","vaultid":"24dbddaa-2c14-4f0e-8189-ac6816449312","uId":"7ee33c58-59d5-488f-bec1-074d1e3ae6b3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A marathon is a long-distance running race that is 26.2 miles long.</li>\n<li>An ultramarathon is a long-distance running race of more than 26.2 miles.</li>\n<li>The Kepler Challenge is a one-day, 37.3-mile ultramarathon in New Zealand.</li>\n<li>The Spreelauf is a six-day, 261-mile ultramarathon in Germany.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to make a generalization about ultramarathons. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d4e03f0a-c538-4752-b0f4-39f1623df16d","content":"<p>Examples of ultramarathons include the 37.3-mile Kepler Challenge in New Zealand and the 261-mile Spreelauf in Germany.</p>"},{"id":"bc403ebb-71dc-4b19-bd88-945ebfb1eb6d","content":"<p>A marathon is 26.2 miles long, but the Spreelauf ultramarathon, at 261 miles, is far longer.</p>"},{"id":"4872fe45-c48b-42dc-9779-df6bc08a0ce5","content":"<p>Ultramarathons range widely in length, from a few dozen miles to a few hundred.</p>"},{"id":"b62c61ec-39cb-40b5-8c34-b81929304a06","content":"<p>While the Kepler Challenge is a one-day ultramarathon, the Spreelauf is a six-day ultramarathon.</p>"}],"keys":["4872fe45-c48b-42dc-9779-df6bc08a0ce5"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. This is the only choice that makes a generalization about ultramarathons. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice gives specific examples of ultramarathons but doesn&rsquo;t say anything about them as a category. Choice B is incorrect. This choice compares marathons in general to one specific ultramarathon but doesn&rsquo;t say anything about ultramarathons as a category. Choice D is incorrect. This choice contrasts two specific ultramarathons but doesn&rsquo;t say anything about them as a category. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-SYN-M007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b37a2d06-cb10-482a-b784-20ab8cb333a5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959372,"updateDate":1691007959372}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959372, 1691007959372),
    ($SATQ$827afb27$SATQ$, $SATQ$022a092a-5b1f-44de-a3fd-af3b8f11340b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e43b0cc9-d061-42d2-aaa7-bbe5c101ee8c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Most conifers (trees belonging to the phylum Coniferophyta)<em> </em>are evergreen. That is, they keep their green leaves or needles year-round. However, not all conifer species are evergreen. Larch trees, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> lose their needles every fall.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c6b19e23-2c3f-46cf-9348-11ede4807e43","content":"<p>for instance,</p>"},{"id":"8ec5c6eb-f53d-4c99-87a9-a9f1b946c801","content":"<p>nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"d0f93925-8437-4376-a03e-325108a1c7f9","content":"<p>meanwhile,</p>"},{"id":"fb2014e6-5fb3-494e-9b5c-369d19bde22f","content":"<p>in addition,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c6b19e23-2c3f-46cf-9348-11ede4807e43"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;For instance&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that larch trees lose their needles every fall&mdash;is an example supporting the claim in the previous sentence (that not all conifer species keep their leaves or needles year-round).&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is true in spite of the claim about conifer species in the previous sentence. Instead, it&rsquo;s an example supporting that claim. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;meanwhile&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is separate from (while occurring simultaneously with) the claim about conifer species in the previous sentence. Instead, it&rsquo;s an example supporting that claim. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;in addition&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is merely an additional fact related to the claim about conifer species in the previous sentence. Instead, it&rsquo;s an example supporting that claim. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-233-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0b9f3b23-7a6a-4046-9974-245908ea6006$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"827afb27","external_id":"022a092a-5b1f-44de-a3fd-af3b8f11340b","vaultid":"e43b0cc9-d061-42d2-aaa7-bbe5c101ee8c","uId":"2d1daa0f-9e05-4d6b-ad2c-6c423fd23343","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Most conifers (trees belonging to the phylum Coniferophyta)<em> </em>are evergreen. That is, they keep their green leaves or needles year-round. However, not all conifer species are evergreen. Larch trees, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> lose their needles every fall.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c6b19e23-2c3f-46cf-9348-11ede4807e43","content":"<p>for instance,</p>"},{"id":"8ec5c6eb-f53d-4c99-87a9-a9f1b946c801","content":"<p>nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"d0f93925-8437-4376-a03e-325108a1c7f9","content":"<p>meanwhile,</p>"},{"id":"fb2014e6-5fb3-494e-9b5c-369d19bde22f","content":"<p>in addition,</p>"}],"keys":["c6b19e23-2c3f-46cf-9348-11ede4807e43"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;For instance&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that larch trees lose their needles every fall&mdash;is an example supporting the claim in the previous sentence (that not all conifer species keep their leaves or needles year-round).&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is true in spite of the claim about conifer species in the previous sentence. Instead, it&rsquo;s an example supporting that claim. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;meanwhile&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is separate from (while occurring simultaneously with) the claim about conifer species in the previous sentence. Instead, it&rsquo;s an example supporting that claim. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;in addition&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is merely an additional fact related to the claim about conifer species in the previous sentence. Instead, it&rsquo;s an example supporting that claim. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-233-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0b9f3b23-7a6a-4046-9974-245908ea6006","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959333,"updateDate":1691007959333}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959333, 1691007959333),
    ($SATQ$82ec9628$SATQ$, $SATQ$285e3025-d702-4d3b-a5f7-d9162ecdc5f7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3fa60e1d-abe4-43f6-9f29-88b6b1eb358f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Archaeologist Sue Brunning explains why the seventh-century ship burial site at Sutton Hoo in England was likely the tomb of a king. First, the gold artifacts inside the ship suggest that the person buried with them was a wealthy and respected leader. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the massive effort required to bury the ship would likely only have been undertaken for a king.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b0d53f55-3546-4797-8c10-8c50efa5a940","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"3040fd5f-2d75-4095-ba1e-a550a94202d1","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"f099b584-e6ee-446b-bc3f-434dde60d493","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"b50d7bbd-5ff6-473a-b6c6-aa3bf4e1eba1","content":"<p>Second,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b50d7bbd-5ff6-473a-b6c6-aa3bf4e1eba1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Second&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the effort to bury the ship would likely only have been made for a king&mdash;joins the information in the previous sentence (&ldquo;first&hellip;&rdquo;) in supporting Brunning&rsquo;s claim that the burial site was likely the tomb of a king. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;instead&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence presents an alternative or substitute to the previous information about the gold artifacts inside the ship. Rather, this sentence presents a second piece of information that supports Brunning&rsquo;s claim. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;still&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence exists in contrast to or despite the previous information about the gold artifacts inside the ship. Instead, this sentence presents a second piece of information that supports Brunning&rsquo;s claim. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;specifically&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence specifies or elaborates on the previous information about the gold artifacts inside the ship. Instead, this sentence presents a second piece of information that supports Brunning&rsquo;s claim. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-221-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$145fb3a2-9ed8-47af-a71e-9bb5b8ec63a3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"82ec9628","external_id":"285e3025-d702-4d3b-a5f7-d9162ecdc5f7","vaultid":"3fa60e1d-abe4-43f6-9f29-88b6b1eb358f","uId":"c32e7c39-1002-488d-841e-d4df098f3784","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Archaeologist Sue Brunning explains why the seventh-century ship burial site at Sutton Hoo in England was likely the tomb of a king. First, the gold artifacts inside the ship suggest that the person buried with them was a wealthy and respected leader. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the massive effort required to bury the ship would likely only have been undertaken for a king.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b0d53f55-3546-4797-8c10-8c50efa5a940","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"3040fd5f-2d75-4095-ba1e-a550a94202d1","content":"<p>Still,</p>"},{"id":"f099b584-e6ee-446b-bc3f-434dde60d493","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"b50d7bbd-5ff6-473a-b6c6-aa3bf4e1eba1","content":"<p>Second,</p>"}],"keys":["b50d7bbd-5ff6-473a-b6c6-aa3bf4e1eba1"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Second&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the effort to bury the ship would likely only have been made for a king&mdash;joins the information in the previous sentence (&ldquo;first&hellip;&rdquo;) in supporting Brunning&rsquo;s claim that the burial site was likely the tomb of a king. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;instead&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence presents an alternative or substitute to the previous information about the gold artifacts inside the ship. Rather, this sentence presents a second piece of information that supports Brunning&rsquo;s claim. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;still&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence exists in contrast to or despite the previous information about the gold artifacts inside the ship. Instead, this sentence presents a second piece of information that supports Brunning&rsquo;s claim. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;specifically&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence specifies or elaborates on the previous information about the gold artifacts inside the ship. Instead, this sentence presents a second piece of information that supports Brunning&rsquo;s claim. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-221-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"145fb3a2-9ed8-47af-a71e-9bb5b8ec63a3","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959332,"updateDate":1691007959332}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959332, 1691007959332),
    ($SATQ$835b101b$SATQ$, $SATQ$7e339545-8459-496d-8249-48f3cef8f795$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$47f7027a-ac54-407c-ad07-8ae4e43c71f4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Minnesota defines a lake as an inland body of water of at least 10 acres.</li>
<li>Wisconsin&rsquo;s definition of a lake doesn&rsquo;t take size into account.</li>
<li>By its own definition, Wisconsin has over 15,000 lakes, many smaller than 10 acres.</li>
<li>By Minnesota&rsquo;s definition, Wisconsin has only about 6,000 lakes.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to contrast Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake with Wisconsin&rsquo;s. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f515fd43-9094-44b0-b6ac-9c0155ea6c64","content":"<p>Wisconsin, which doesn&rsquo;t take size into account in defining a lake, claims that it has over 15,000 lakes.</p>"},{"id":"61f2a874-0bd9-41e4-8af9-78ed19f148ba","content":"<p>Because its definition of a lake is different from Minnesota&rsquo;s, it is unclear how many lakes Wisconsin really has.</p>"},{"id":"a786885f-9772-485c-93ad-a68e745f50fa","content":"<p>According to Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake&mdash;an inland body of water of at least 10 acres&mdash;Wisconsin has about 6,000 lakes.</p>"},{"id":"9c9892ad-864d-4ed2-b8bd-f0a24735bee4","content":"<p>Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake&mdash;an inland body of water of at least 10 acres&mdash;is more restrictive than Wisconsin&rsquo;s, which doesn&rsquo;t take size into account.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9c9892ad-864d-4ed2-b8bd-f0a24735bee4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence contrasts Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake with Wisconsin&rsquo;s, explaining that Minnesota&rsquo;s definition (which takes size into account) is more restrictive than Wisconsin&rsquo;s definition (which doesn&rsquo;t). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence notes that Wisconsin&rsquo;s definition of a lake doesn&rsquo;t take size into account, it doesn&rsquo;t contrast Minnesota&rsquo;s definition with Wisconsin&rsquo;s. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence states that Wisconsin&rsquo;s definition of a lake is different from Minnesota&rsquo;s, but it doesn&rsquo;t clarify how they differ. In other words, it doesn&rsquo;t contrast Minnesota&rsquo;s definition with Wisconsin&rsquo;s. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence indicates how many lakes Wisconsin has according to Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake, but it doesn&rsquo;t clarify how the states&rsquo; definitions differ. In other words, it doesn&rsquo;t contrast Minnesota&rsquo;s definition with Wisconsin&rsquo;s. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-755-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e58b3929-7d8a-45a4-95fe-cec99eb56154$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"835b101b","external_id":"7e339545-8459-496d-8249-48f3cef8f795","vaultid":"47f7027a-ac54-407c-ad07-8ae4e43c71f4","uId":"f5bc5731-c377-4467-bdb0-8cabf600ba6c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minnesota defines a lake as an inland body of water of at least 10 acres.</li>\n<li>Wisconsin&rsquo;s definition of a lake doesn&rsquo;t take size into account.</li>\n<li>By its own definition, Wisconsin has over 15,000 lakes, many smaller than 10 acres.</li>\n<li>By Minnesota&rsquo;s definition, Wisconsin has only about 6,000 lakes.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to contrast Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake with Wisconsin&rsquo;s. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f515fd43-9094-44b0-b6ac-9c0155ea6c64","content":"<p>Wisconsin, which doesn&rsquo;t take size into account in defining a lake, claims that it has over 15,000 lakes.</p>"},{"id":"61f2a874-0bd9-41e4-8af9-78ed19f148ba","content":"<p>Because its definition of a lake is different from Minnesota&rsquo;s, it is unclear how many lakes Wisconsin really has.</p>"},{"id":"a786885f-9772-485c-93ad-a68e745f50fa","content":"<p>According to Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake&mdash;an inland body of water of at least 10 acres&mdash;Wisconsin has about 6,000 lakes.</p>"},{"id":"9c9892ad-864d-4ed2-b8bd-f0a24735bee4","content":"<p>Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake&mdash;an inland body of water of at least 10 acres&mdash;is more restrictive than Wisconsin&rsquo;s, which doesn&rsquo;t take size into account.</p>"}],"keys":["9c9892ad-864d-4ed2-b8bd-f0a24735bee4"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence contrasts Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake with Wisconsin&rsquo;s, explaining that Minnesota&rsquo;s definition (which takes size into account) is more restrictive than Wisconsin&rsquo;s definition (which doesn&rsquo;t). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence notes that Wisconsin&rsquo;s definition of a lake doesn&rsquo;t take size into account, it doesn&rsquo;t contrast Minnesota&rsquo;s definition with Wisconsin&rsquo;s. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence states that Wisconsin&rsquo;s definition of a lake is different from Minnesota&rsquo;s, but it doesn&rsquo;t clarify how they differ. In other words, it doesn&rsquo;t contrast Minnesota&rsquo;s definition with Wisconsin&rsquo;s. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence indicates how many lakes Wisconsin has according to Minnesota&rsquo;s definition of a lake, but it doesn&rsquo;t clarify how the states&rsquo; definitions differ. In other words, it doesn&rsquo;t contrast Minnesota&rsquo;s definition with Wisconsin&rsquo;s. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-755-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e58b3929-7d8a-45a4-95fe-cec99eb56154","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959351,"updateDate":1691007959351}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959351, 1691007959351),
    ($SATQ$8432a140$SATQ$, $SATQ$dc8038cf-bd93-438e-9b0d-d0539c79e24d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b4244c53-19f7-4f31-8031-44c0b8e402b7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Marine biologist Camille Jazmin Gaynus studies coral reefs.</li>
<li>Coral reefs are vital underwater ecosystems that provide habitats to 25% of all marine species.</li>
<li>Reefs can include up to 8,000 species of fish, such as toadfish, seahorses, and clown triggerfish.</li>
<li>The Amazon Reef is a coral reef in Brazil.</li>
<li>It is one of the largest known reefs in the world.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to introduce the scientist and her field of study to a new audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a30d4b2a-fd44-4582-af30-7760c1b80791","content":"<p>Located in Brazil, the Amazon Reef is one of the largest known coral reefs in the world.</p>"},{"id":"288c97ba-6dc8-42ef-824c-c5b791ecfc9e","content":"<p>Marine biologist Camille Jazmin Gaynus studies coral reefs, vital underwater ecosystems that provide homes to 25% of all marine species.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"19a66c39-b0a4-4534-a74f-9e1e18c047c5","content":"<p>Providing homes to 25% of all marine species, including up to 8,000 species of fish, coral reefs are vital underwater ecosystems and thus of great interest to marine biologists.</p>"},{"id":"6c572884-b83f-4f79-8e7a-17b79125c6cc","content":"<p>As Camille Jazmin Gaynus knows well, coral reefs are vital underwater ecosystems, providing homes to thousands of species of fish.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["288c97ba-6dc8-42ef-824c-c5b791ecfc9e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. We&rsquo;re asked to select the sentence that "introduce[s] the scientist and her field of study." This choice introduces scientist Camille Jazmin Gaynus as a marine biologist and explains what marine life she studies.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t mention Camille Jazmin Gaynus, so it fails to "introduce the scientist." Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t mention Camille Jazmin Gaynus, so it fails to "introduce the scientist." Choice D is incorrect. This choice mentions Camille Jazmin Gaynus, but it doesn&rsquo;t identify her as a marine biologist. It says she "knows well" about coral reefs, but doesn&rsquo;t identify her expertise as a "field of study."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-718-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$38849e5f-0e29-4077-a846-8722daf5455f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"8432a140","external_id":"dc8038cf-bd93-438e-9b0d-d0539c79e24d","vaultid":"b4244c53-19f7-4f31-8031-44c0b8e402b7","uId":"456604c4-9d13-4093-b9cd-32b9ec88c742","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marine biologist Camille Jazmin Gaynus studies coral reefs.</li>\n<li>Coral reefs are vital underwater ecosystems that provide habitats to 25% of all marine species.</li>\n<li>Reefs can include up to 8,000 species of fish, such as toadfish, seahorses, and clown triggerfish.</li>\n<li>The Amazon Reef is a coral reef in Brazil.</li>\n<li>It is one of the largest known reefs in the world.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to introduce the scientist and her field of study to a new audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a30d4b2a-fd44-4582-af30-7760c1b80791","content":"<p>Located in Brazil, the Amazon Reef is one of the largest known coral reefs in the world.</p>"},{"id":"288c97ba-6dc8-42ef-824c-c5b791ecfc9e","content":"<p>Marine biologist Camille Jazmin Gaynus studies coral reefs, vital underwater ecosystems that provide homes to 25% of all marine species.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"19a66c39-b0a4-4534-a74f-9e1e18c047c5","content":"<p>Providing homes to 25% of all marine species, including up to 8,000 species of fish, coral reefs are vital underwater ecosystems and thus of great interest to marine biologists.</p>"},{"id":"6c572884-b83f-4f79-8e7a-17b79125c6cc","content":"<p>As Camille Jazmin Gaynus knows well, coral reefs are vital underwater ecosystems, providing homes to thousands of species of fish.</p>"}],"keys":["288c97ba-6dc8-42ef-824c-c5b791ecfc9e"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. We&rsquo;re asked to select the sentence that \"introduce[s] the scientist and her field of study.\" This choice introduces scientist Camille Jazmin Gaynus as a marine biologist and explains what marine life she studies.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t mention Camille Jazmin Gaynus, so it fails to \"introduce the scientist.\" Choice C is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t mention Camille Jazmin Gaynus, so it fails to \"introduce the scientist.\" Choice D is incorrect. This choice mentions Camille Jazmin Gaynus, but it doesn&rsquo;t identify her as a marine biologist. It says she \"knows well\" about coral reefs, but doesn&rsquo;t identify her expertise as a \"field of study.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-718-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"38849e5f-0e29-4077-a846-8722daf5455f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$84e108cf$SATQ$, $SATQ$3b370a85-148d-45df-9982-78f63bc51a53$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f9b006ff-b1d1-4ae8-8e9a-0dfa2d733ec1$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Platinum is a rare and expensive metal.</li>
<li>It is used as a catalyst for chemical reactions.</li>
<li>Platinum catalysts typically require a large amount of platinum to be effective.</li>
<li>Researcher Jianbo Tang and his colleagues created a platinum catalyst that combines platinum with liquid gallium.</li>
<li>Their catalyst was highly effective and required only trace amounts of platinum (0.0001% of the atoms in the mixture).</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to explain an advantage of the new platinum catalyst developed by Jianbo Tang and his colleagues. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"568bdf4a-02d2-433f-bf46-321528ea1285","content":"<p>Researcher Jianbo Tang and his colleagues created a platinum catalyst that combines platinum, a rare and expensive metal, with liquid gallium.</p>"},{"id":"2d731d70-8537-4425-b576-94c92f7c70fc","content":"<p>Like other platinum catalysts, the new platinum catalyst requires a particular amount of the metal to be effective.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"fe2b6768-5e05-43a9-b2e4-036e6a76ada8","content":"<p>Platinum is a rare and expensive metal that is used as a catalyst for chemical reactions; however, platinum catalysts typically require a large amount of platinum to be effective.</p>"},{"id":"60d29cae-e8c7-48a0-953b-573af110563f","content":"<p>While still highly effective, the new platinum catalyst requires far less of the rare and expensive metal than do other platinum catalysts.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["60d29cae-e8c7-48a0-953b-573af110563f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence explains an advantage of Tang and his colleagues&rsquo; platinum catalyst, noting that it requires far less platinum (which is rare and expensive) than other platinum catalysts do. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes the platinum catalyst that Tang and his colleagues created; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of their platinum catalyst. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the new platinum catalyst and other platinum catalysts; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the new platinum catalyst. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence connects the metal platinum to the functioning of platinum catalysts, noting that large amounts of platinum are typically required for platinum catalysts to be effective; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of Tang and his colleagues&rsquo; platinum catalyst. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-SYN-M003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$fa1520af-1800-48c1-9455-51a17a783f16$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"84e108cf","external_id":"3b370a85-148d-45df-9982-78f63bc51a53","vaultid":"f9b006ff-b1d1-4ae8-8e9a-0dfa2d733ec1","uId":"1275cfad-52a6-4548-96dd-65bff34ec5bb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Platinum is a rare and expensive metal.</li>\n<li>It is used as a catalyst for chemical reactions.</li>\n<li>Platinum catalysts typically require a large amount of platinum to be effective.</li>\n<li>Researcher Jianbo Tang and his colleagues created a platinum catalyst that combines platinum with liquid gallium.</li>\n<li>Their catalyst was highly effective and required only trace amounts of platinum (0.0001% of the atoms in the mixture).</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to explain an advantage of the new platinum catalyst developed by Jianbo Tang and his colleagues. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"568bdf4a-02d2-433f-bf46-321528ea1285","content":"<p>Researcher Jianbo Tang and his colleagues created a platinum catalyst that combines platinum, a rare and expensive metal, with liquid gallium.</p>"},{"id":"2d731d70-8537-4425-b576-94c92f7c70fc","content":"<p>Like other platinum catalysts, the new platinum catalyst requires a particular amount of the metal to be effective.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"fe2b6768-5e05-43a9-b2e4-036e6a76ada8","content":"<p>Platinum is a rare and expensive metal that is used as a catalyst for chemical reactions; however, platinum catalysts typically require a large amount of platinum to be effective.</p>"},{"id":"60d29cae-e8c7-48a0-953b-573af110563f","content":"<p>While still highly effective, the new platinum catalyst requires far less of the rare and expensive metal than do other platinum catalysts.</p>"}],"keys":["60d29cae-e8c7-48a0-953b-573af110563f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence explains an advantage of Tang and his colleagues&rsquo; platinum catalyst, noting that it requires far less platinum (which is rare and expensive) than other platinum catalysts do. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes the platinum catalyst that Tang and his colleagues created; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of their platinum catalyst. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the new platinum catalyst and other platinum catalysts; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the new platinum catalyst. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence connects the metal platinum to the functioning of platinum catalysts, noting that large amounts of platinum are typically required for platinum catalysts to be effective; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of Tang and his colleagues&rsquo; platinum catalyst. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-SYN-M003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"fa1520af-1800-48c1-9455-51a17a783f16","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959372,"updateDate":1691007959372}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959372, 1691007959372),
    ($SATQ$855247c7$SATQ$, $SATQ$caa9e207-e30f-41ec-b353-c35b7b9b9175$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Mary Ellen Pleasant, a successful entrepreneur during the gold rush era, earned the moniker &ldquo;Mother of Human Rights in California&rdquo; after successfully challenging discrimination in the state. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in 1866, she sued a streetcar company for denying her and other Black riders service, a suit she eventually won when the California Supreme Court declared it illegal for carriers to exclude passengers based on race.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8f89de97-2a4f-4e81-89a1-afbbaa7dca32","content":"<p>For this reason,</p>"},{"id":"14187df2-4c40-45c0-a9f6-2c4d8fe04038","content":"<p>Then,</p>"},{"id":"ace078c2-c6d4-41e3-acf1-367d957e521e","content":"<p>In addition,</p>"},{"id":"1d8a0f43-1f95-4b45-9fb2-8193d552278e","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1d8a0f43-1f95-4b45-9fb2-8193d552278e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "Specifically" logically signals that the information about Pleasant&rsquo;s 1866 lawsuit provides specific, precise details elaborating on the previous point that Pleasant successfully challenged discrimination in California.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "for this reason" illogically signals that Pleasant&rsquo;s 1866 lawsuit was a result of her successful challenge to discrimination in California. Instead, this sentence provides specific details elaborating on her challenge to discrimination in the state. Choice B is incorrect because "then" illogically signals that Pleasant&rsquo;s 1866 lawsuit was subsequent to or resulted from her successful challenge to discrimination in California. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on how she challenged discrimination in the state. Choice C is incorrect because "in addition" illogically signals that the information about Pleasant&rsquo;s 1866 lawsuit is merely additional to the previous point that Pleasant successfully challenged discrimination in California. Instead, this sentence provides specific details elaborating on how she did so.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"855247c7","external_id":"caa9e207-e30f-41ec-b353-c35b7b9b9175","vaultid":null,"uId":"e83416ef-d424-4d36-b1b2-a3c203cadf64","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Mary Ellen Pleasant, a successful entrepreneur during the gold rush era, earned the moniker &ldquo;Mother of Human Rights in California&rdquo; after successfully challenging discrimination in the state. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in 1866, she sued a streetcar company for denying her and other Black riders service, a suit she eventually won when the California Supreme Court declared it illegal for carriers to exclude passengers based on race.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8f89de97-2a4f-4e81-89a1-afbbaa7dca32","content":"<p>For this reason,</p>"},{"id":"14187df2-4c40-45c0-a9f6-2c4d8fe04038","content":"<p>Then,</p>"},{"id":"ace078c2-c6d4-41e3-acf1-367d957e521e","content":"<p>In addition,</p>"},{"id":"1d8a0f43-1f95-4b45-9fb2-8193d552278e","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"}],"keys":["1d8a0f43-1f95-4b45-9fb2-8193d552278e"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"Specifically\" logically signals that the information about Pleasant&rsquo;s 1866 lawsuit provides specific, precise details elaborating on the previous point that Pleasant successfully challenged discrimination in California.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"for this reason\" illogically signals that Pleasant&rsquo;s 1866 lawsuit was a result of her successful challenge to discrimination in California. Instead, this sentence provides specific details elaborating on her challenge to discrimination in the state. Choice B is incorrect because \"then\" illogically signals that Pleasant&rsquo;s 1866 lawsuit was subsequent to or resulted from her successful challenge to discrimination in California. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on how she challenged discrimination in the state. Choice C is incorrect because \"in addition\" illogically signals that the information about Pleasant&rsquo;s 1866 lawsuit is merely additional to the previous point that Pleasant successfully challenged discrimination in California. Instead, this sentence provides specific details elaborating on how she did so.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$85c0c0f0$SATQ$, $SATQ$e939fb1f-d91a-4052-8f19-299e0acaa1b9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Texture analysis and historical analysis are two approaches to art criticism.</li>
<li>Texture analysis examines how surfaces are visually represented in an artwork.</li>
<li>Such an analysis of Giorgione&rsquo;s <em>Youth Holding an Arrow</em> might consider how the painting&rsquo;s blended colors make the subject&rsquo;s skin appear smooth in texture.</li>
<li>Historical analysis considers the historical context in which a work was created.</li>
<li>Such an analysis of Diego Vel&aacute;zquez&rsquo;s <em>Las Meninas</em> might consider how the painting&rsquo;s depiction of the artist with King Philip IV symbolizes art&rsquo;s historical ties to power.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"768ec28f-6b6b-4b27-bb20-03a7c6f80c2f","content":"<p>A texture analysis of <em>Youth Holding an Arrow</em>&nbsp;might consider how the painting&rsquo;s blended colors make the subject&rsquo;s skin appear smooth in texture.</p>"},{"id":"7cdb569c-95b9-4696-b3a2-d13b4a61b1d6","content":"<p>Texture analysis differs from historical analysis in that texture analysis examines how surfaces are visually represented in an artwork.</p>"},{"id":"76e5ff5b-4441-4f97-ac3f-400223918aea","content":"<p>An approach to art criticism, historical analysis considers the historical context in which a work was created.</p>"},{"id":"e461bab5-3d79-49f7-8ab5-0b9451cec17c","content":"<p><em>Las Meninas</em>&rsquo;s depiction of the artist with King Philip IV symbolizes art&rsquo;s historical ties to power.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["76e5ff5b-4441-4f97-ac3f-400223918aea"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence presents historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept by defining it as an approach to art criticism that considers the historical context in which a work was created.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it provides an example of texture analysis; it doesn&rsquo;t present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence explains a difference between texture analysis and historical analysis; it doesn&rsquo;t present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept. Choice D is incorrect. It provides an example of historical analysis; it doesn&rsquo;t present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"85c0c0f0","external_id":"e939fb1f-d91a-4052-8f19-299e0acaa1b9","vaultid":null,"uId":"62b27399-e2d8-4cb2-933a-de5d5ebb1922","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Texture analysis and historical analysis are two approaches to art criticism.</li>\n<li>Texture analysis examines how surfaces are visually represented in an artwork.</li>\n<li>Such an analysis of Giorgione&rsquo;s <em>Youth Holding an Arrow</em> might consider how the painting&rsquo;s blended colors make the subject&rsquo;s skin appear smooth in texture.</li>\n<li>Historical analysis considers the historical context in which a work was created.</li>\n<li>Such an analysis of Diego Vel&aacute;zquez&rsquo;s <em>Las Meninas</em> might consider how the painting&rsquo;s depiction of the artist with King Philip IV symbolizes art&rsquo;s historical ties to power.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"768ec28f-6b6b-4b27-bb20-03a7c6f80c2f","content":"<p>A texture analysis of <em>Youth Holding an Arrow</em>&nbsp;might consider how the painting&rsquo;s blended colors make the subject&rsquo;s skin appear smooth in texture.</p>"},{"id":"7cdb569c-95b9-4696-b3a2-d13b4a61b1d6","content":"<p>Texture analysis differs from historical analysis in that texture analysis examines how surfaces are visually represented in an artwork.</p>"},{"id":"76e5ff5b-4441-4f97-ac3f-400223918aea","content":"<p>An approach to art criticism, historical analysis considers the historical context in which a work was created.</p>"},{"id":"e461bab5-3d79-49f7-8ab5-0b9451cec17c","content":"<p><em>Las Meninas</em>&rsquo;s depiction of the artist with King Philip IV symbolizes art&rsquo;s historical ties to power.</p>"}],"keys":["76e5ff5b-4441-4f97-ac3f-400223918aea"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence presents historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept by defining it as an approach to art criticism that considers the historical context in which a work was created.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it provides an example of texture analysis; it doesn&rsquo;t present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence explains a difference between texture analysis and historical analysis; it doesn&rsquo;t present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept. Choice D is incorrect. It provides an example of historical analysis; it doesn&rsquo;t present historical analysis to an audience unfamiliar with the concept.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061192,"updateDate":1755117061192}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061192, 1755117061192),
    ($SATQ$8622320e$SATQ$, $SATQ$55990205-1650-49d6-925b-5ca9a3c1a14b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4357c8a2-dccb-45cb-b39e-b3218adce1d0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Earth&rsquo;s auroras&mdash;colorful displays of light seen above the northern and southern poles&mdash;result, broadly speaking, from the Sun&rsquo;s activity. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the Sun releases charged particles that are captured by Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field and channeled toward the poles. These particles then collide with atoms in the atmosphere, causing the atoms to emit auroral light.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9f7bdfb2-393f-423d-a20d-c7f1a66308a6","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"c6e9027d-65df-4803-86d0-864852d1e07f","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"9b29c6c8-b030-4c6c-a1a7-89b5a1226649","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"6ed12ed6-d26c-44eb-9746-e5f22f43829c","content":"<p>Hence,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9f7bdfb2-393f-423d-a20d-c7f1a66308a6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Specifically&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the Sun releases charged particles that later collide with atoms, resulting in auroral light&mdash;provides specific, precise details about how auroras result from the Sun&rsquo;s activity.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;similarly&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is similar to the general information about auroras in the previous sentence. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details about how auroras form. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is despite the general information about auroras in the previous sentence. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details about how auroras form. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;hence&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result of the general information about auroras in the previous sentence. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details about how auroras form. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-205-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f96c5b78-5617-4a36-a06e-e7f70a03fec4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"8622320e","external_id":"55990205-1650-49d6-925b-5ca9a3c1a14b","vaultid":"4357c8a2-dccb-45cb-b39e-b3218adce1d0","uId":"83f522ee-dc33-4534-9bfc-f8fe3e680461","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Earth&rsquo;s auroras&mdash;colorful displays of light seen above the northern and southern poles&mdash;result, broadly speaking, from the Sun&rsquo;s activity. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the Sun releases charged particles that are captured by Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field and channeled toward the poles. These particles then collide with atoms in the atmosphere, causing the atoms to emit auroral light.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9f7bdfb2-393f-423d-a20d-c7f1a66308a6","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"c6e9027d-65df-4803-86d0-864852d1e07f","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"9b29c6c8-b030-4c6c-a1a7-89b5a1226649","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"6ed12ed6-d26c-44eb-9746-e5f22f43829c","content":"<p>Hence,</p>"}],"keys":["9f7bdfb2-393f-423d-a20d-c7f1a66308a6"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Specifically&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the Sun releases charged particles that later collide with atoms, resulting in auroral light&mdash;provides specific, precise details about how auroras result from the Sun&rsquo;s activity.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;similarly&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is similar to the general information about auroras in the previous sentence. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details about how auroras form. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is despite the general information about auroras in the previous sentence. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details about how auroras form. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;hence&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result of the general information about auroras in the previous sentence. Instead, this sentence provides specific, precise details about how auroras form. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-205-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f96c5b78-5617-4a36-a06e-e7f70a03fec4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959325,"updateDate":1691007959325}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959325, 1691007959325),
    ($SATQ$86b78078$SATQ$, $SATQ$77881421-3e40-4139-8342-2d1cb10f3af0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d6c90b8e-6669-43cf-8982-7739e998cff0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Samuel Selvon was a Trinidadian author.</li>
<li><em>The Lonely Londoners </em>is one of his most celebrated novels.</li>
<li>Selvon published the novel in 1956.</li>
<li>It is about a group of men who emigrate from the Caribbean to Great Britain after World War II.</li>
<li>Some of <em>The Lonely Londoners</em>&rsquo; characters also appear in Selvon&rsquo;s later novel <em>Moses Ascending</em>.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to introduce Samuel Selvon and his novel <em>The Lonely Londoners </em>to a new audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c6001397-9897-472e-bf4d-1ad4f51413ac","content":"<p>In 1956, Trinidadian author Samuel Selvon published one of his most celebrated novels, <em>The Lonely Londoners, </em>which is about a group of men who emigrate from the Caribbean to Great Britain after World War II.</p>"},{"id":"ba05df17-68ac-4a81-81c7-0be1011d5738","content":"<p>Samuel Selvon wrote the novel&nbsp;<em>Moses Ascending&nbsp;</em>after he wrote&nbsp;<em>The Lonely Londoners.&nbsp;</em></p>"},{"id":"6cd19d5d-45c7-4c5f-88ef-4efaade211d6","content":"<p><em>The Lonely Londoners</em>, a celebrated novel that was published in 1956, depicts post&ndash;World War II Caribbean migration from the perspective of a Trinidadian author.</p>"},{"id":"3faa8930-6006-4ed5-b0a3-e95f504d90a1","content":"<p>Some of the characters who appear in Samuel Selvon&rsquo;s <em>Moses Ascending </em>also appear in <em>The Lonely Londoners. </em></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c6001397-9897-472e-bf4d-1ad4f51413ac"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. By noting that Selvon is a Trinidadian author and indicating that <em>The Lonely Londoners</em>, published in 1956, is about a group of men who emigrate from the Caribbean to Great Britain after World War II, the sentence effectively introduces Samuel Selvon and his novel to a new audience. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence indicates the order in which two of Selvon&rsquo;s novels were written; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce Samuel Selvon and <em>The Lonely Londoners</em> to a new audience. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence describes the novel <em>The Lonely Londoners</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t mention its author, Samuel Selvon, by name and thus doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce him to a new audience. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence indicates that two of Selvon&rsquo;s novels include the same characters; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce Samuel Selvon and <em>The Lonely Londoners</em> to a new audience. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-769-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$952e71f8-3db6-48a7-903e-fcb9f6edfaaa$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"86b78078","external_id":"77881421-3e40-4139-8342-2d1cb10f3af0","vaultid":"d6c90b8e-6669-43cf-8982-7739e998cff0","uId":"d8d83807-07dd-4b42-836e-dbd7ea2ea191","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Samuel Selvon was a Trinidadian author.</li>\n<li><em>The Lonely Londoners </em>is one of his most celebrated novels.</li>\n<li>Selvon published the novel in 1956.</li>\n<li>It is about a group of men who emigrate from the Caribbean to Great Britain after World War II.</li>\n<li>Some of <em>The Lonely Londoners</em>&rsquo; characters also appear in Selvon&rsquo;s later novel <em>Moses Ascending</em>.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to introduce Samuel Selvon and his novel <em>The Lonely Londoners </em>to a new audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c6001397-9897-472e-bf4d-1ad4f51413ac","content":"<p>In 1956, Trinidadian author Samuel Selvon published one of his most celebrated novels, <em>The Lonely Londoners, </em>which is about a group of men who emigrate from the Caribbean to Great Britain after World War II.</p>"},{"id":"ba05df17-68ac-4a81-81c7-0be1011d5738","content":"<p>Samuel Selvon wrote the novel&nbsp;<em>Moses Ascending&nbsp;</em>after he wrote&nbsp;<em>The Lonely Londoners.&nbsp;</em></p>"},{"id":"6cd19d5d-45c7-4c5f-88ef-4efaade211d6","content":"<p><em>The Lonely Londoners</em>, a celebrated novel that was published in 1956, depicts post&ndash;World War II Caribbean migration from the perspective of a Trinidadian author.</p>"},{"id":"3faa8930-6006-4ed5-b0a3-e95f504d90a1","content":"<p>Some of the characters who appear in Samuel Selvon&rsquo;s <em>Moses Ascending </em>also appear in <em>The Lonely Londoners. </em></p>"}],"keys":["c6001397-9897-472e-bf4d-1ad4f51413ac"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. By noting that Selvon is a Trinidadian author and indicating that <em>The Lonely Londoners</em>, published in 1956, is about a group of men who emigrate from the Caribbean to Great Britain after World War II, the sentence effectively introduces Samuel Selvon and his novel to a new audience. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence indicates the order in which two of Selvon&rsquo;s novels were written; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce Samuel Selvon and <em>The Lonely Londoners</em> to a new audience. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence describes the novel <em>The Lonely Londoners</em>, it doesn&rsquo;t mention its author, Samuel Selvon, by name and thus doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce him to a new audience. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence indicates that two of Selvon&rsquo;s novels include the same characters; it doesn&rsquo;t introduce Samuel Selvon and <em>The Lonely Londoners</em> to a new audience. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-769-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"952e71f8-3db6-48a7-903e-fcb9f6edfaaa","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959351,"updateDate":1691007959351}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959351, 1691007959351),
    ($SATQ$878f835a$SATQ$, $SATQ$07538f6f-969e-4a62-98cb-db4cf3b65b2e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:&nbsp;</p>
<ul>
<li>Some atoms contain an excess of neutrons.</li>
<li>Often, these neutrons form a &ldquo;skin&rdquo; on the atom&rsquo;s surface.</li>
<li>An atom of lead-208 has a neutron skin.</li>
<li>The thickness of its neutron skin is approximately 0.28 trillionths of a millimeter.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the thickness of lead-208&rsquo;s neutron skin. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bf8bffb1-a301-45b7-8fab-cf599410dabd","content":"<p>The neutron skin surrounding an atom of lead-208 measures about 0.28 trillionths of a millimeter.</p>"},{"id":"787c42e6-8690-4689-a72c-46d92b40f73b","content":"<p>Atoms with excess neutrons will often acquire a neutron skin.</p>"},{"id":"67c4c8c3-e9ea-4195-813a-90078b8ea2d0","content":"<p>An atom of lead-208, like some other atoms, is surrounded by a neutron skin.</p>"},{"id":"4cc57dc8-a407-4028-ab48-a5bef8147d06","content":"<p>Neutrons surround the surface of an atom of lead-208.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["bf8bffb1-a301-45b7-8fab-cf599410dabd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the thickness of lead-208&rsquo;s neutron skin, noting that it is about 0.28 trillionths of a millimeter thick.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence makes a generalization about atoms, stating that atoms with excess neutrons will often acquire a neutron skin; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the thickness of lead-208&rsquo;s neutron skin. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence states that lead-208 has a neutron skin; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the thickness of that skin. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence indicates that lead-208 is surrounded by neutrons; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the thickness of lead-208&rsquo;s neutron skin.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"878f835a","external_id":"07538f6f-969e-4a62-98cb-db4cf3b65b2e","vaultid":null,"uId":"41860bcd-f8ff-424c-a100-0414de147f27","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:&nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some atoms contain an excess of neutrons.</li>\n<li>Often, these neutrons form a &ldquo;skin&rdquo; on the atom&rsquo;s surface.</li>\n<li>An atom of lead-208 has a neutron skin.</li>\n<li>The thickness of its neutron skin is approximately 0.28 trillionths of a millimeter.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the thickness of lead-208&rsquo;s neutron skin. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bf8bffb1-a301-45b7-8fab-cf599410dabd","content":"<p>The neutron skin surrounding an atom of lead-208 measures about 0.28 trillionths of a millimeter.</p>"},{"id":"787c42e6-8690-4689-a72c-46d92b40f73b","content":"<p>Atoms with excess neutrons will often acquire a neutron skin.</p>"},{"id":"67c4c8c3-e9ea-4195-813a-90078b8ea2d0","content":"<p>An atom of lead-208, like some other atoms, is surrounded by a neutron skin.</p>"},{"id":"4cc57dc8-a407-4028-ab48-a5bef8147d06","content":"<p>Neutrons surround the surface of an atom of lead-208.</p>"}],"keys":["bf8bffb1-a301-45b7-8fab-cf599410dabd"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the thickness of lead-208&rsquo;s neutron skin, noting that it is about 0.28 trillionths of a millimeter thick.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence makes a generalization about atoms, stating that atoms with excess neutrons will often acquire a neutron skin; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the thickness of lead-208&rsquo;s neutron skin. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence states that lead-208 has a neutron skin; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the thickness of that skin. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence indicates that lead-208 is surrounded by neutrons; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the thickness of lead-208&rsquo;s neutron skin.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$87d34a39$SATQ$, $SATQ$47825dd9-caac-44a1-b61d-8a36623c6ece$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The National Congress of American Indians (NCAI) was founded in 1944 by representatives of fifty tribal governments.</li>
<li>The NCAI was created to protect the sovereignty of Indigenous tribes.</li>
<li>Napoleon B. Johnson (Cherokee) was the NCAI&rsquo;s first president.</li>
<li>In 1975, the US Congress passed the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act (Public Law 96-638).</li>
<li>This legislation formally acknowledged tribes&rsquo; right to self-governance.</li>
<li>The advocacy of the NCAI was a key factor in the law&rsquo;s passing.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to identify an accomplishment of the NCAI. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"01a55931-0f91-48a5-94b1-a729c371cbd8","content":"<p>The NCAI, founded by representatives of fifty tribal governments, had Napoleon B. Johnson (Cherokee) as its first president.</p>"},{"id":"84e4c206-5b79-43bd-b7a2-9d0547929ded","content":"<p>Founded in 1944, the NCAI was created by representatives of tribal governments from fifty sovereign Indigenous tribes. &nbsp; &nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"377c7c97-ff80-4d8c-9b41-bd696ea5020f","content":"<p>The NCAI&rsquo;s advocacy was key to the passing of Public Law 96-638, legislation formally acknowledging Indigenous tribes&rsquo; right to self-governance.</p>"},{"id":"13cdcf06-eab2-4f28-8a51-bdc36ae5bd79","content":"<p>In 1975, the NCAI passed the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act, which was created to protect the sovereignty of Indigenous tribes.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["377c7c97-ff80-4d8c-9b41-bd696ea5020f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence directly identifies an accomplishment of the NCAI: that its advocacy was key to the passing of Public Law 96-638, which formally acknowledged Indigenous tribes&rsquo; right to self-governance.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence provides information about the NCAI&rsquo;s founding and its first president, but it doesn&rsquo;t identify an accomplishment of the organization. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes the NCAI&rsquo;s founding, but it doesn&rsquo;t identify an accomplishment of the organization. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents the information in the notes: the US Congress, not the NCAI, passed the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"87d34a39","external_id":"47825dd9-caac-44a1-b61d-8a36623c6ece","vaultid":null,"uId":"ff427655-44aa-4468-a99b-b71b141d0b59","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The National Congress of American Indians (NCAI) was founded in 1944 by representatives of fifty tribal governments.</li>\n<li>The NCAI was created to protect the sovereignty of Indigenous tribes.</li>\n<li>Napoleon B. Johnson (Cherokee) was the NCAI&rsquo;s first president.</li>\n<li>In 1975, the US Congress passed the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act (Public Law 96-638).</li>\n<li>This legislation formally acknowledged tribes&rsquo; right to self-governance.</li>\n<li>The advocacy of the NCAI was a key factor in the law&rsquo;s passing.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to identify an accomplishment of the NCAI. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"01a55931-0f91-48a5-94b1-a729c371cbd8","content":"<p>The NCAI, founded by representatives of fifty tribal governments, had Napoleon B. Johnson (Cherokee) as its first president.</p>"},{"id":"84e4c206-5b79-43bd-b7a2-9d0547929ded","content":"<p>Founded in 1944, the NCAI was created by representatives of tribal governments from fifty sovereign Indigenous tribes. &nbsp; &nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"377c7c97-ff80-4d8c-9b41-bd696ea5020f","content":"<p>The NCAI&rsquo;s advocacy was key to the passing of Public Law 96-638, legislation formally acknowledging Indigenous tribes&rsquo; right to self-governance.</p>"},{"id":"13cdcf06-eab2-4f28-8a51-bdc36ae5bd79","content":"<p>In 1975, the NCAI passed the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act, which was created to protect the sovereignty of Indigenous tribes.</p>"}],"keys":["377c7c97-ff80-4d8c-9b41-bd696ea5020f"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence directly identifies an accomplishment of the NCAI: that its advocacy was key to the passing of Public Law 96-638, which formally acknowledged Indigenous tribes&rsquo; right to self-governance.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence provides information about the NCAI&rsquo;s founding and its first president, but it doesn&rsquo;t identify an accomplishment of the organization. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes the NCAI&rsquo;s founding, but it doesn&rsquo;t identify an accomplishment of the organization. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents the information in the notes: the US Congress, not the NCAI, passed the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061177,"updateDate":1755117061177}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061177, 1755117061177),
    ($SATQ$87d8a2ff$SATQ$, $SATQ$f93a02d8-826b-49c5-aed8-a019e194a3ad$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$c1d2e578-b2c2-4ac6-80a6-c40337fe33f8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>During a 2021 launch, Rocket Labs&rsquo; Electron rocket experienced an unexpected failure: its second-stage booster shut down suddenly after ignition. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> instead of downplaying the incident, Rocket Labs&rsquo; CEO publicly acknowledged what happened and apologized for the loss of the rocket&rsquo;s payload, which had consisted of two satellites.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"30990ddf-b04f-4eb1-a2d4-e5e5dab0584c","content":"<p>Afterward,</p>"},{"id":"c1fccbed-e6dd-4060-bd10-d5e5cd92950c","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"dd02a62d-82de-483c-b0f7-4d9be3eca716","content":"<p>Indeed,</p>"},{"id":"34c46831-717c-4f51-bd8d-07de0344e9d1","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["30990ddf-b04f-4eb1-a2d4-e5e5dab0584c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Afterward&rdquo; logically signals that the events described in this sentence&mdash;the CEO&rsquo;s public acknowledgment and apology&mdash;occurred after the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure and are part of a chronological sequence of events. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;additionally&rdquo; illogically signals that the events described in this sentence merely occurred in addition to the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure. Instead, they occurred after the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure and are part of a chronological sequence of events. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;indeed&rdquo; illogically signals that the events described in this sentence emphasize or strengthen a statement made in the previous sentence. Instead, they occurred after the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure and are part of a chronological sequence of events. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;similarly&rdquo; illogically signals that the events described in this sentence are similar to the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure. Instead, they occurred after the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure and are part of a chronological sequence of events. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-209-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$ffe959c7-26cf-44e9-b5d9-490264304409$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"87d8a2ff","external_id":"f93a02d8-826b-49c5-aed8-a019e194a3ad","vaultid":"c1d2e578-b2c2-4ac6-80a6-c40337fe33f8","uId":"e82354c6-3653-4875-bc28-2f84fc535944","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>During a 2021 launch, Rocket Labs&rsquo; Electron rocket experienced an unexpected failure: its second-stage booster shut down suddenly after ignition. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> instead of downplaying the incident, Rocket Labs&rsquo; CEO publicly acknowledged what happened and apologized for the loss of the rocket&rsquo;s payload, which had consisted of two satellites.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"30990ddf-b04f-4eb1-a2d4-e5e5dab0584c","content":"<p>Afterward,</p>"},{"id":"c1fccbed-e6dd-4060-bd10-d5e5cd92950c","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"dd02a62d-82de-483c-b0f7-4d9be3eca716","content":"<p>Indeed,</p>"},{"id":"34c46831-717c-4f51-bd8d-07de0344e9d1","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"}],"keys":["30990ddf-b04f-4eb1-a2d4-e5e5dab0584c"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Afterward&rdquo; logically signals that the events described in this sentence&mdash;the CEO&rsquo;s public acknowledgment and apology&mdash;occurred after the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure and are part of a chronological sequence of events. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;additionally&rdquo; illogically signals that the events described in this sentence merely occurred in addition to the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure. Instead, they occurred after the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure and are part of a chronological sequence of events. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;indeed&rdquo; illogically signals that the events described in this sentence emphasize or strengthen a statement made in the previous sentence. Instead, they occurred after the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure and are part of a chronological sequence of events. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;similarly&rdquo; illogically signals that the events described in this sentence are similar to the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure. Instead, they occurred after the rocket booster&rsquo;s failure and are part of a chronological sequence of events. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-209-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ffe959c7-26cf-44e9-b5d9-490264304409","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959326,"updateDate":1691007959326}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959326, 1691007959326),
    ($SATQ$88308a39$SATQ$, $SATQ$5d8bd209-24bb-401d-89ce-1316ae245b5d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$eb6d9cc6-b3a1-4bdb-8148-1c51a7fe508d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Shaun Tan is an Australian author.</li>
<li>In 2008, he published <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia</em>, a book of fifteen short stories.</li>
<li>The stories describe surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary suburban neighborhoods.</li>
<li>In 2018, he published <em>Tales from the Inner City</em>, a book of twenty-five short stories.</li>
<li>The stories describe surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary urban settings.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two books by Shaun Tan. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f31437ee-0850-41f9-b178-d52058ad96e1","content":"<p>Shaun Tan&rsquo;s book <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia</em>, which describes surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary places,<em> </em>contains fewer short stories than <em>Tales from the Inner City </em>does.</p>"},{"id":"aa2e39be-19a7-43b8-99d2-9d4988372b5b","content":"<p><em>Tales from Outer Suburbia </em>was published in 2008, and <em>Tales from the Inner City </em>was published in&nbsp;2018.</p>"},{"id":"d966f729-3c50-4c9c-991b-4f7fcf1b91b4","content":"<p>Unlike <em>Tales from the Inner City</em>, Shaun Tan&rsquo;s book <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia </em>is set in suburban neighborhoods.</p>"},{"id":"786cf96b-ccb8-499e-9e41-5299e0013798","content":"<p>Shaun Tan&rsquo;s books <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia </em>and <em>Tales from the Inner City </em>both describe surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary places.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["786cf96b-ccb8-499e-9e41-5299e0013798"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence uses &ldquo;both&rdquo; to emphasize a thematic similarity between Tan&rsquo;s two books, noting that both <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia</em> and <em>Tales from the Inner City </em>describe surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary places. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a difference (one contains fewer stories than the other), not a similarity, between the two books.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Tan&rsquo;s books were published ten years apart; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two books. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence uses &ldquo;unlike&rdquo; to emphasize a difference between <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia</em> and <em>Tales from the Inner City</em>; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two books. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-739-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$234fbea8-ac68-43a1-bf80-7c7f87708783$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"88308a39","external_id":"5d8bd209-24bb-401d-89ce-1316ae245b5d","vaultid":"eb6d9cc6-b3a1-4bdb-8148-1c51a7fe508d","uId":"7575dd0f-c4cb-42a2-9af9-3fb5eaff7633","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shaun Tan is an Australian author.</li>\n<li>In 2008, he published <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia</em>, a book of fifteen short stories.</li>\n<li>The stories describe surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary suburban neighborhoods.</li>\n<li>In 2018, he published <em>Tales from the Inner City</em>, a book of twenty-five short stories.</li>\n<li>The stories describe surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary urban settings.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two books by Shaun Tan. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f31437ee-0850-41f9-b178-d52058ad96e1","content":"<p>Shaun Tan&rsquo;s book <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia</em>, which describes surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary places,<em> </em>contains fewer short stories than <em>Tales from the Inner City </em>does.</p>"},{"id":"aa2e39be-19a7-43b8-99d2-9d4988372b5b","content":"<p><em>Tales from Outer Suburbia </em>was published in 2008, and <em>Tales from the Inner City </em>was published in&nbsp;2018.</p>"},{"id":"d966f729-3c50-4c9c-991b-4f7fcf1b91b4","content":"<p>Unlike <em>Tales from the Inner City</em>, Shaun Tan&rsquo;s book <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia </em>is set in suburban neighborhoods.</p>"},{"id":"786cf96b-ccb8-499e-9e41-5299e0013798","content":"<p>Shaun Tan&rsquo;s books <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia </em>and <em>Tales from the Inner City </em>both describe surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary places.</p>"}],"keys":["786cf96b-ccb8-499e-9e41-5299e0013798"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence uses &ldquo;both&rdquo; to emphasize a thematic similarity between Tan&rsquo;s two books, noting that both <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia</em> and <em>Tales from the Inner City </em>describe surreal events occurring in otherwise ordinary places. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a difference (one contains fewer stories than the other), not a similarity, between the two books.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect. The sentence indicates that Tan&rsquo;s books were published ten years apart; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two books. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence uses &ldquo;unlike&rdquo; to emphasize a difference between <em>Tales from Outer Suburbia</em> and <em>Tales from the Inner City</em>; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize a similarity between the two books. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-739-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"234fbea8-ac68-43a1-bf80-7c7f87708783","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$883493d5$SATQ$, $SATQ$e27fbdf6-d244-477f-91b5-804e24e6bf28$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ab8ddddc-6acd-43cb-851f-b03e35241323$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Allan Houser was a Chiricahua Warm Springs Apache sculptor, illustrator, and painter.</li>
<li>Many of his sculptures featured Native American figures.</li>
<li>He depicted this subject matter using abstract, modernist forms, developing a distinctive style that influenced many other artists.</li>
<li>His well-known sculpture <em>Sacred Rain Arrow</em> was pictured on the State of Oklahoma license plate.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to describe the distinctive style of Houser&rsquo;s sculptures. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b8a7af4c-8326-462f-bf45-a4105bcf6a08","content":"<p>A sculptor, illustrator, and painter, Houser developed a distinctive style for portraying Native American figures.</p>"},{"id":"f7a8978d-3fab-4c73-b85e-ffba29e885ca","content":"<p>Houser&rsquo;s sculptures employ abstract, modernist forms to depict Native American figures.</p>"},{"id":"77c4792a-20ea-4da5-92b2-108af1912760","content":"<p>Many other artists have been influenced by the style of Houser&rsquo;s sculptures.</p>"},{"id":"dc234525-3fec-4ef2-839d-dd113c1d278d","content":"<p>The sculpture <em>Sacred Rain Arrow</em> is a well-known example of Houser&rsquo;s style.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f7a8978d-3fab-4c73-b85e-ffba29e885ca"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence describes the distinctive style of Houser&rsquo;s sculptures, explaining that the sculptures use abstract, modernist forms to depict Native American figures. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence indicates that Houser developed a distinctive style for portraying Native American figures, it doesn&rsquo;t describe this style. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence states that other artists have been influenced by the style of Houser&rsquo;s sculptures, it doesn&rsquo;t describe this style. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence mentions the name of a sculpture that&rsquo;s a well-known example of Houser&rsquo;s style, it doesn&rsquo;t describe the sculpture&rsquo;s style. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-749-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$631c4335-f768-410e-aa79-306fb06e4dc8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"883493d5","external_id":"e27fbdf6-d244-477f-91b5-804e24e6bf28","vaultid":"ab8ddddc-6acd-43cb-851f-b03e35241323","uId":"22212193-6303-4b54-a0e0-f27123e40a22","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Allan Houser was a Chiricahua Warm Springs Apache sculptor, illustrator, and painter.</li>\n<li>Many of his sculptures featured Native American figures.</li>\n<li>He depicted this subject matter using abstract, modernist forms, developing a distinctive style that influenced many other artists.</li>\n<li>His well-known sculpture <em>Sacred Rain Arrow</em> was pictured on the State of Oklahoma license plate.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to describe the distinctive style of Houser&rsquo;s sculptures. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b8a7af4c-8326-462f-bf45-a4105bcf6a08","content":"<p>A sculptor, illustrator, and painter, Houser developed a distinctive style for portraying Native American figures.</p>"},{"id":"f7a8978d-3fab-4c73-b85e-ffba29e885ca","content":"<p>Houser&rsquo;s sculptures employ abstract, modernist forms to depict Native American figures.</p>"},{"id":"77c4792a-20ea-4da5-92b2-108af1912760","content":"<p>Many other artists have been influenced by the style of Houser&rsquo;s sculptures.</p>"},{"id":"dc234525-3fec-4ef2-839d-dd113c1d278d","content":"<p>The sculpture <em>Sacred Rain Arrow</em> is a well-known example of Houser&rsquo;s style.</p>"}],"keys":["f7a8978d-3fab-4c73-b85e-ffba29e885ca"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence describes the distinctive style of Houser&rsquo;s sculptures, explaining that the sculptures use abstract, modernist forms to depict Native American figures. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence indicates that Houser developed a distinctive style for portraying Native American figures, it doesn&rsquo;t describe this style. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence states that other artists have been influenced by the style of Houser&rsquo;s sculptures, it doesn&rsquo;t describe this style. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence mentions the name of a sculpture that&rsquo;s a well-known example of Houser&rsquo;s style, it doesn&rsquo;t describe the sculpture&rsquo;s style. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-749-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"631c4335-f768-410e-aa79-306fb06e4dc8","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$8b2636ee$SATQ$, $SATQ$382bc7c4-7c28-4110-a7d8-d4efed037dad$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Angana Chaudhuri is a scientist.</li>
<li>Chaudhuri studies sedimentary rocks.</li>
<li>A scientist who studies sedimentary rocks is called a sedimentologist.</li>
<li>Shale, chalk, and sandstone are examples of sedimentary rocks.</li>
